[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a one-line edit ([38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m in [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m), but it changes a security boundary, so I
  want a confirmation before making it rather than after.

  What a 16× longer session lifetime means here:
  - A stolen or leaked session token stays valid for a full working day instead of half an hour. On a shared or
    unattended machine, an abandoned session stays live all day.
  - [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m is the only consumer, and there is no idle-vs-absolute distinction in this config — 480 minutes becomes
    the total session life regardless of activity, with no re-auth in between.
  - If any compliance baseline applies (PCI DSS caps idle sessions at 15 minutes; many SOC 2 / internal policies
    use 30), 8 hours is outside it.

  A common middle path is keeping a short idle timeout with a long absolute cap plus sliding renewal, which gets the
  "don't log me out while I'm working" benefit without an 8-hour window on a stolen token. That would be a design change
  rather than a value change, though.
